  [1mClassification: bounded[0m — the login flow already exists here, one function and one caller, so I'll present a short
  design in chat rather than write a spec. Say the word if you'd rather I go heavier.

  One thing I need to settle before touching the signature, because it changes what the parameter is worth:

  [1mThe fork:[0m nothing in the app currently has a user id at the moment [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is called. The caller only has what the
  user typed. A user's id is normally something you [3mlearn from[0m authenticating, not something you hand to the
  authenticator — so "add a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter" needs a source, and the three plausible sources give you genuinely
  different things.

  - [1mCorrelation id generated per attempt[0m — a random id minted in the submit handler and passed in, so every attempt
    (including failures) is traceable. Buys you real tracking today with no backend and no PII. Costs: it identifies the
    attempt/browser, not the person. Cheap to swap later for a real id.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m returned from [38;5;153mlogin[39m, not passed in[0m — stub an id into the return value and log it after the call. Buys
    correctness: identity as an output of auth, which is where it'll come from once there's a real API. Costs: it's a
    return-shape change, not the parameter you asked for, and anything reading [38;5;153mresult[39m sees a new field.
  - [1mLiteral [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter, caller passes the username[0m — exactly the change as worded, one line, signature
    [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m. Costs: [38;5;153muserId[39m and [38;5;153musername[39m are the same value, so the parameter carries no new
    information, and a real id later means changing the signature a second time. Hardest of the three to undo, because
    the signature is the thing others would code against.

  [1mMy recommendation: the correlation id.[0m It's the only one that actually delivers "track who logged in" with the data
  the app has, it keeps the signature honest (the param means something the other arguments don't), and it survives a
  real backend — the server can join its own user id to your correlation id later.

